{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- The player that is better developed has an advantage in open positions. If you are better developed, then open up the center!
- When up a queen, don't worry too much about losing a few pawns if you are able to develop instead.

Stream footage here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=33EpuPv4ULw
Game played here: www.chess.com/live/game/5710916249 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2. Qf3 { The Danya soul read on the scholar's mate. } { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 3. Bc4 { Scholar's mate threat, what should black do? } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rc4f7,Rf3f7] [%clk 0:04:56] } 3... Nf6 { Black can simply develop while stopping the mating threat. } { [%clk 0:04:43] } 4. Qb3 { White threatening winning a pawn. How should black respond? } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rc4f7] [%clk 0:04:54] } 4... Nd4 { Black can simply ignore that threat and respond with one of their own. The idea here is to overload the queen and prevent white from defending the bishop after Bxf7. } { [%csl Rb3][%cal Rd4b3] [%clk 0:04:03] } (4... Na5 5. Bxf7+ Ke7 6. Qb4+ { White's most straightforward option here is to check and... } 6... Kxf7 7. Qxa5 { ... trade pieces. }) 5. Qd3 { Black has a developmental lead, how can black take advantage of this? } { [%clk 0:04:20] } (5. Bxf7+ Ke7 { Note that when you allow Bxf7 in this setup, make sure e6 is defended, otherwise Qe6 is mate! } { [%csl Be6] } 6. Qc4 b5 { Forcing the queen off the diagonal defending the king. } { [%csl Be6,Bd5,Bc4,Bb3] } 7. Qc3 Kxf7 { And black wins a piece. }) 5... d5 { By opening the center! } { [%clk 0:03:37] } 6. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:03] } 6... Bf5 { Forcing the queen away from defending the forking square. } { [%csl Rd3,Rc2,Re1,Ra1][%cal Rf5d3,Rd4c2,Rc2e1,Rc2a1] [%clk 0:03:26] } 7. Qxf5?? { Or not... } { [%csl Rf5][%cal Rd4f5] [%clk 0:03:43] } 7... Nxf5 { White simply blunders the queen. } { [%clk 0:03:21] } 8. d3 { [%clk 0:03:22] } 8... Nxd5 { [%clk 0:03:16] } 9. Nf3 { Oh no the pawn is under attack! } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rf3e5] [%clk 0:03:13] } 9... Bc5 { Black can ignore this threat and continue to develop. When up a queen, it is okay to give up some pawns for development! } { [%clk 0:02:47] } (9... Bb4+ { Obviously black is winning here, but this allows white some free development opportunities. } 10. c3 Bc5 11. Nxe5 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd3d4] }) 10. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:03] } 10... O-O { Black continuing to follow the plan of development. } { [%clk 0:02:44] } 11. Nf3 { Black chooses not to castle and opens the king up for... } { [%clk 0:02:53] } 11... Re8+ { [%clk 0:02:34] } 12. Be3 { [%clk 0:02:33] } 12... Nfxe3 { Black happy to trade. } { [%clk 0:02:30] } 13. fxe3 { Which piece should black take with? } { [%csl Re3][%cal Rd5e3,Rc5e3,Re8e3] [%clk 0:02:31] } 13... Nxe3 { The knight! Setting up a nasty discovery. } { [%csl Re1][%cal Re8e1] [%clk 0:02:16] } 14. Kd2 { White walking into a pin allowing... } { [%csl Rd2][%cal Rd8d2] [%clk 0:02:19] } 14... Nxc4+ { ... black to win the bishop. } { [%csl Rd2][%cal Rd8d2] [%clk 0:02:03] } 0-1


