{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- When your opponent plays a somewhat dubious opening, like the Philidor, adhere to basic principles to grind out an edge. Playing against these openings requires careful play as they're not easily refuted!
- Advantages should be thought of as "transformational". How can one convert their advantages into a concrete victory?

Games referenced:
Spassky - Geller (1968): https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1049394

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/33EpuPv4ULw?t=445
Game played here: www.chess.com/live/game/5710935107 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2... d6 { Black opts for the Philidor.
Fun fact: Philidor, the chess player, never played this opening. } { [%clk 0:04:47] } 3. d4 { White tries to open the center as black's development is hampered with the dark squared bishop being blocked. } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf8d6] [%clk 0:04:44] } 3... exd4 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 4. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 4... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 5. Nc3 { White can ignore the threat of Nxd4 as the queen is defending the knight and simply continue development. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc6d4,Gd1d4] [%clk 0:04:35] } 5... Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:39] } 6. Qxd4 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 6... Bd7 { How does white continue their development? } { [%clk 0:04:30] } 7. Bc4 { Placing the light squared bishop on an active square. } { [%clk 0:04:12] } 7... Ne7 { [%clk 0:04:09] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:07] } 8... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:05] } 9. Qd1 { Seems like a passive move for white, but this gets the queen to a safe square and allows it to redeploy. } { [%clk 0:03:45] } (9. Qd5 { This further exposes the queen and allows black to get some free tempi. } 9... Be6) (9. Qd3 { Same idea here, black can gain some free tempi and... } 9... Ne5 { ... force white to trade their active bishop for the knight. } { [%csl Rc4,Rd3][%cal Re5c4,Re5d3] }) 9... Be7 { [%clk 0:04:02] } 10. Nd5 { Moving the knight onto that juicy (mostly) outpost on d5. } { [%clk 0:03:19] } 10... O-O { [%clk 0:03:51] } 11. f4 { White starting a kingside attack after black castles. } { [%clk 0:03:12] } 11... Be6 { How can white finish development? } { [%clk 0:03:48] } 12. Bd2 { Seems passive at first, but will allow the dark squared bishop to maneuver to a better square. } { [%csl Ga5][%cal Gd2a5] [%clk 0:02:41] } (12. b3 { This seems like a good idea to fianchetto the dark squared bishop. However... } { [%csl Gb2][%cal Gc1b2] } 12... Bf6 { ... this weakens the long diagonal for white and Bf6 gives control of it to black. } { [%csl Ra1][%cal Rf6a1] }) 12... Na5?? { Black blundering the knight. } { [%clk 0:03:41] } (12... Bxd5 { White does not mind losing the knight outpost as it can simply be replaced with the bishop. } 13. Bxd5 { Controlling a nice diagonal here. } { [%csl Rg8][%cal Rd5g8] }) 13. Bxa5 { [%clk 0:02:40] } 13... b6 { [%clk 0:03:39] } 14. Bc3 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 14... c6 { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc6d5] [%clk 0:03:33] } 15. Qd4 { White threatening checkmate with a bishop queen battery. } { [%csl Rg7][%cal Rc3g7] [%clk 0:02:11] } 15... cxd5?? { [%clk 0:03:27] } 16. Qxg7# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } { Black stumbles into checkmate. } { [%clk 0:02:10] } 1-0


