{ THEMES and Things to Look For
- The knight and the queen are among the most powerful combination of attacking pieces.

Games referenced:
Naroditsky - Varuzhan (2011): https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1621001

Stream footage here: https://youtu.be/33EpuPv4ULw?t=731
Game played here: www.chess.com/live/game/5710952030 }
1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 1... e6 { Start of the French Defense. } { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2... d5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. e5 { Going into the Advance French line which closes the position } { [%clk 0:04:51] } 3... f6 { Black opts to attack the tip of the pawn chain and not the base. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rf6e5] [%clk 0:04:57] } 4. exf6 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 4... Nxf6 { Note the e5 square has potential to be a juicy outpost for white. } { [%csl Be5] [%clk 0:04:55] } 5. Nf3 { Ideas of eventually getting to e5 with the knight. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gf3e5] [%clk 0:04:20] } 5... Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 6. Bd3 { There's no need to immediately outpost the knight, white can continue developing for now. } { [%clk 0:04:14] } (6. Ne5 { Playing this immediately allows black to trade and force white to fill the weak square "cavity" with a pawn rather than a more active piece. } 6... Bxe5 7. dxe5) 6... Nbd7 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:04:10] } 7... b6 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 8. Re1 { White taking control of the half open e file and attacking the undefended pawn } { [%csl Re6,Re8][%cal Re1e8] [%clk 0:03:49] } 8... Nf8 { White would like to remove this bishop so the e5 square is ripe for outpost. } { [%csl Ge6,Bd6][%cal Gf8e6] [%clk 0:04:49] } 9. Bg5 { Developing the bishop to an active square. A common idea in the French is to maneuver the dark squared bishop to g3 to invite the trade such that the e5 square is clear for white's knight to outpost on. } { [%csl Rd8][%cal Rg5d8,Gg5h4,Gh4g3] [%clk 0:03:12] } (9. Bf4 { Probably obvious, but white cannot immediately play Bf4 because it hangs the bishop. } 9... Bxf4) (9. g3 { Preparing the trade via g3 is not advisable because it exposes white's king at the end of the exchange. } 9... Bb7 10. Bf4 Bxf4 11. gxf4) 9... Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 10. Bh4 { Continuing the manouver } { [%csl Gg3][%cal Gh4g3] [%clk 0:03:07] } 10... Be7 { Black does white's bidding and allows Ne5. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gf3e5] [%clk 0:04:33] } 11. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:05] } 11... Ng6 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 12. Bxg6+ { Opening up the kingside for a potential attack. } { [%clk 0:02:41] } 12... hxg6 { [%clk 0:04:24] } 13. Qd3 { Hanging the bishop for... } { [%csl Rg6,Rh4][%cal Rd3g6,Rh8h4] [%clk 0:02:27] } 13... Rxh4 { [%clk 0:04:07] } (13... Rh6 { Black's attempt to defend. } { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gh6g6] } 14. Bg5 { But after this black is lost as black will be unable to stop mate and retain material. } { [%csl Rh6][%cal Rg5h6] } 14... Ng8 15. Bxh6 Nxh6 16. Qxg6+ Kf8 { The knight may be stopping checkmate, but white has won an exchange and has a crippling attack going. } { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gh6f7] }) 14. Qxg6+ { ... a forced mate. } { [%clk 0:02:26] } 14... Kf8 { [%clk 0:04:04] } 15. Qf7# { 1-0 White wins by checkmate. } { [%clk 0:02:26] } 1-0


